= Formal language =

== Overview ==
A '''formal language''' is a mathematically specified set of finite strings over an alphabet.

Formal languages are central to automata theory, compiler construction, logic, and the formal description of programming-language syntax.

== Alphabet and strings ==
An '''alphabet''' is a finite set of symbols.

A '''string''' is a finite sequence of symbols from the alphabet.

If Σ is an alphabet, Σ* denotes the set of all finite strings over Σ, including the empty string.

A formal language L over Σ is then:

<pre>
L ⊆ Σ*
</pre>

== Defining languages ==
A language can be specified by:

* listing its strings when finite;
* a grammar;
* a regular expression;
* an automaton that recognizes it;
* a logical or algebraic condition;
* an algorithm deciding membership.

Different descriptions can define the same language.

== Language and grammar ==
A grammar is a system of production rules that generates strings.

The language is the set of strings generated.

Therefore:

<pre>
formal language ≠ grammar
</pre>

== Formal and natural language ==
Natural languages have rich semantic, pragmatic, social, and historical structure.

Formal languages are deliberately specified mathematical objects.

Natural-language fragments can be modeled formally, but a formal language is not the same thing as human language.

== Programming languages ==
Programming languages have formal syntactic layers, but a programming language also includes semantics, implementation conventions, libraries, tooling, and often effects beyond a bare set of strings.

